# Append-only logs: take both sides instead of conflicting.
#
# CLAUDE.md requires every branch to append to these two files — a red-team critic
# pass before any architecture decision, and a skill observation whenever guidance
# proves wrong. Doing what the guide asks therefore guaranteed a merge conflict:
# every branch appends at the tail, so every branch collides there with every other.
#
# On 2026-07-26 all three of the live conflicting PRs (#219, #220, #221) conflicted
# on redteam.md and nothing else. Each was a pure append — 19, 146 and 19 lines
# added, zero deleted. The conflicts carried no information; they were an artifact of
# two branches growing the same list.
#
# `merge=union` is a git built-in that keeps both sides' lines. It suits these files
# precisely because the edits are append-only: with no deletions there is nothing for
# union to mangle.
#
# Caveat, so nobody is surprised: union is line-based and structure-blind. If two
# branches ever *edit* the same existing entry rather than appending a new one, union
# keeps both versions and you get a duplicated passage instead of a conflict marker
# telling you to choose. For a growing log that trade is worth it; do not extend
# merge=union to source files, where a silent both-sides merge is how you get code
# that compiles and is wrong.
.agent-reviews/redteam.md merge=union
skill-observations/observation-log.md merge=union
